diagnosticQuestions.question:
A teacher shows you the following partial Java program and asks you to complete it so that it prints "Namaste, Class 8" on the screen. Complete the missing parts and explain each one.
```
class ________ {
    public static void main(_______[]) {
        ____________________;
    }
}
```

diagnosticQuestions.answer:
The completed program should be:
```
class Namaste {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Namaste, Class 8");
    }
}
```
The first blank is the class name, which we choose to be `Namaste`. This name should also be the file name, so the file must be saved as `Namaste.java`. The second blank is `String`, which is the data type of the array that the main method receives as arguments. Although at this stage we do not use the argument, the exact signature `String[] args` is required. The third blank is the statement that prints the message, `System.out.println("Namaste, Class 8");`. Notice the opening and closing double quotes around the text, the round brackets around the message, and the semicolon at the end. The braces match in pairs: one for the class and one for the main method. If the file is named `Namaste.java` and all spelling and punctuation are exactly as shown, the program will compile and run, printing the required message.

Here is the full program:
```
class IndependenceGreeting {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Happy Independence Day, NPS Bengaluru!");
        System.out.println("Let us celebrate 15th August with pride.");
    }
}
```
The file must be saved as `IndependenceGreeting.java` because the class name and file name must match exactly. The `class IndependenceGreeting {` line is the class declaration, and it is like the envelope or the outermost boundary of the program. In flowchart terms, the whole program is the diagram, and the class is the page that holds it. The `public static void main(String[] args)` line is the main method. It is the START oval of the flowchart, because execution begins there. The two `System.out.println` statements are the parallelogram input/output symbols of the flowchart, because they produce output on the screen. Each statement ends with a semicolon, which is like the full stop at the end of a sentence. The braces `{ }` are the boxes that group the steps: one pair groups everything in the class, and one pair groups everything in the main method. When the program runs, the computer will start at the main method and print the two messages one after another, just as the flowchart would flow from the START oval through two output parallelograms.

A complete Java program to print the first eight Fibonacci terms is shown below. We use an int variable n set to the literal value 8. Two int variables a and b start at 0 and 1, and a for loop runs from i = 1 to i <= n. Inside the loop the program prints the current value of a followed by a space, then it calculates the next term in a third variable c and moves a and b forward.

class Fibonacci {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 8;
        int a = 0, b = 1;
        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i = i + 1) {
            System.out.print(a + " ");
            int c = a + b;
            a = b;
            b = c;
        }
    }
}

The output is 0 1 1 2 3 5 8 13. The trace begins with a = 0 and b = 1. In the first iteration i is 1, the program prints 0, then c becomes 0 + 1 = 1, a becomes 1, and b becomes 1. In the second iteration i is 2, it prints 1, c becomes 1 + 1 = 2, a becomes 1, and b becomes 2. In the third iteration i is 3, it prints 1, c becomes 1 + 2 = 3, a becomes 2, and b becomes 3. This swapping of values continues until i reaches 8. After the eighth print, the loop condition i <= 8 becomes false, so the program ends. The variables a and b keep shifting one step ahead each time, which is why each new term is the sum of the two previous terms. The literal 8 appears directly in the code as the fixed number of terms, while a, b, c, and i are variables whose values change.
